  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry weather with warmer than average temperatures through \nTuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will remain to the east of the region. Due to the\ncenter of the high far off to the east, strong enough subsidence\nwill keep the region dry. In addition, wide pressure gradients\nwill keep the winds fairly weak. In combination with the upper\nlevel trof bringing warm southerly air, temps will increase into\nthe low 80s today and Tuesday. Low dewpoints will keep humidity\nlow.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA system is likely to move in later in the week, bringing \nanother round of wetting rainfall into the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAs is often the case, models are trending slower with the timing\nof a developing cutoff low that tracks from somewhere near NE\nPA/SE NY/NW NJ Wednesday to near Long Island by Thursday morning\nas the system becomes vertically stacked. The cutoff low tracks\nslowly ENE into the Gulf of Maine by Friday morning, and into \nthe southern Maritimes by Saturday morning, as the system \ngradually opens up as it weakens. The surface low follows a \nsimilar if not identical track to the upper system from Thursday\ninto Saturday.\n\nMost 00z guidance slows down precipitation until after 12z\nThursday. Do have chance pops over most of the area Wednesday\nnight for now, but this likely is overdone.\n\nThere is some indication that the precipitation instead of being\nwidespread over all of Northern and Eastern Maine, could be more\nconcentrated in the Bangor Region and Downeast Maine than areas\nfurther north. It appears any rain should taper off Friday \nnight or Saturday morning, depending on exactly how quickly the\nstorm exits into the Maritimes (note systems like this do tend \nto pick up speed as they open up). However a widespread rainfall\nlate this week over the entire region cannot yet be ruled out \nfrom Thursday into late Friday night/Saturday morning.",
